{"bill":{"id":"119-s-2715","congress":119,"billType":"s","billNumber":"2715","title":"FAIR Act of 2025","introducedDate":"2025-09-04","latestActionDate":"2025-09-04","latestActionText":"Read twice and referred to the Committee on Finance.","policyArea":"Health","subjects":[],"sponsorBioguideId":"D000618","cosponsorBioguideIds":["H001046","K000383","B001319","T000278","S001232","S001181","O000174"],"committeeCodes":[],"committeeNames":[],"officialSummary":"Fair Access In Residency Act of 2025 or the FAIR Act of 2025 This bill requires hospitals that receive graduate medical education (GME) payments under Medicare to report certain information about the composition of their medical residency training programs. Specifically, hospitals must provide to the Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services (CMS) (1) the number of applicants for residencies who are from osteopathic medical schools and from allopathic medical schools, (2) the number of applicants who are accepted from each of these types of medical schools, and (3) an affirmation that the hospital considers applicants from each of these types of medical schools and accepts specified related examination results, as appropriate. Hospitals that do not provide this information are subject to a 2% reduction in their GME payments. The CMS must post the information it receives on a public website.","congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/senate-bill/2715","source":"congress","sourceUrl":"https://www.govinfo.gov/bulkdata/BILLSTATUS/119/s/BILLSTATUS-119s2715.xml","fetchedAt":"2026-07-29T01:30:10.960Z"},"classification":{"billId":"119-s-2715","plainSummary":"This bill requires hospitals that receive Medicare graduate medical education payments to report how many residency applicants come from osteopathic versus allopathic medical schools, how many from each group are accepted, and to confirm they consider both types of applicants equally. Hospitals that fail to report this information would see their Medicare education payments reduced by 2%. The data would be posted publicly online.","industries":[{"industry":"health-providers","confidence":0.95,"rationale":"Hospitals receiving Medicare GME payments face new reporting requirements and potential payment reductions for noncompliance."},{"industry":"education","confidence":0.65,"rationale":"Medical schools (both osteopathic and allopathic) are directly affected by transparency requirements around residency acceptance rates."}],"method":"llm","model":"claude-sonnet-4-5","inputHash":"73c699120373453c9f8580ee","classifiedAt":"2026-07-30T02:42:03.302Z"},"plain":{"whatItDoes":"This bill would require hospitals that receive graduate medical education payments under Medicare to report certain information about the composition of their medical residency training programs.","whoItTouches":"Anyone who sees a doctor or pays for care.","everydayEffect":"If it passed, the change would show up in getting care, and paying for it. Nothing has changed yet. Most bills never become law.","confidence":"crs-summary","source":"Reworded from the official summary. Staff at the Congressional Research Service wrote that summary. It is public domain, and the full text of it is further down this page.","titleInPlainWords":null},"overlaps":[{"billId":"119-s-2715","bioguideId":"S001181","cycle":2026,"score":0.02228555200078345,"matches":[{"industry":"health-providers","donorAmount":3295,"donorShare":0.019700018049879964,"billConfidence":0.95,"contribution":0.011696885717116226},{"industry":"education","donorAmount":4359.5,"donorShare":0.0260644093136424,"billConfidence":0.65,"contribution":0.010588666283667225}],"method":{"formula":"score = Σ_industries ( donorShare(industry) × billWeight(industry) ), where billWeight = classifierConfidence / Σ classifierConfidence. Range 0–1, read as a weighted share of disclosed itemized money.","donorProfileCycle":2026,"billClassificationMethod":"llm","unclassifiedDonorShare":0.6021164336235245},"member":{